# Gradle & Build Logic

## Version and Dependencies

- Centralize versions and libraries in **gradle/libs.versions.toml**. Do not hardcode versions in module `build.gradle.kts`; use version catalog references (e.g. `libs.kotlin.stdlib`).
- Use the same Kotlin and AGP versions across all modules; align with `libs.versions.toml`.

## Convention Plugins

- Build logic lives in **build-logic** as convention plugins. Application and library modules apply these plugins (e.g. `com.pulsekit.android.application`, `com.pulsekit.android.library`, `com.pulsekit.kotlin.mpp`) instead of duplicating configuration.
- When adding new modules, use the existing convention plugins; extend build-logic only when a new pattern is needed.
- Plugin IDs and properties are defined under `build-logic/src/main/resources/META-INF/gradle-plugins/`.

## Module Layout

- **pulsekit-core**: Kotlin Multiplatform (commonMain, androidMain, jvmMain). Use `KotlinMultiplatformLibraryPlugin` or equivalent.
- **pulsekit-android**: Android library. Use Android library convention plugin.
- **sample-app**: Android application. Use Android application convention plugin.
- Do not add Android dependencies to pulsekit-core; keep it platform-agnostic in commonMain.

## Tasks and CI

- Preserve existing compatibility tasks: `checkApiCompatibility`, `checkBinaryCompatibility`, `updateApiBaselines`. See [docs/ApiCompatibility.md](docs/ApiCompatibility.md).
- Use `gradle.properties` and project properties for flags (e.g. `FORCE_API_COMPATIBILITY`) rather than hardcoding.
- Before commit: run `./gradlew spotlessApply` and fix `./gradlew detekt` if needed. See [.cursor/rules/code-style-lint.mdc](.cursor/rules/code-style-lint.mdc).
